+  "discoveredDate": "2026-09-21",
+  "id": "ISS-004",
+  "impact": "AppLogger's key-based redaction (SECRET_KEY, src/platform/logging/AppLogger.ts:18) only fires on an object KEY, so any secret that arrives inside a flat string is caught only if it matches SECRET_VALUE/SECRET_IN_TEXT (lines 19-20), which recognise exactly two shapes: `sk-...` and `Bearer ...`. Verified by execution during the T-2 code review: a log field whose string value is `{\"token\":\"hunter2secret\",\"api_key\":\"sk_live_abcdefgh\"}` is written verbatim -- the JSON key shape is not a real object key so SECRET_KEY cannot fire, and neither `hunter2secret` nor the `sk_live_` prefix (underscore, not hyphen) matches the value patterns. Other common shapes also pass: `ghp_...`, `xoxb-...`, `AKIA...`, `eyJ...` JWTs, and `password=hunter2` in a query string or connection URL. This is pre-existing and not introduced by T-2, but T-2 widens the exposure: JobQueue's new failure line funnels arbitrary third-party error text (`describeError(err)`) into a log field for the first time, and upstream libraries routinely put request URLs and response bodies into error messages. RULES.md #1 says secrets never reach a log line unredacted, so this is a gap in the rule's enforcement rather than a style question. Fix is to widen the value/text patterns (common token prefixes, `key=value` pairs naming a secret, URL credentials) -- which changes redaction behaviour for every existing caller and so deserves its own ticket and review rather than riding along on a job-traceability change.",
